{
  "summary": "Across all 15 canvases, Phase 3 produces a coherent, detailed collection: all 90 diagrams are connected within their canvases, attachment lines are valid, and supplied text rectangles do not overlap. The main weaknesses are incomplete separation of overview from detail, repetition and inconsistent evidence qualifications across points, and layouts that prioritize rectangle clearance over navigation. These are actionable Phase 3 choices; actual connector routing, clipping, and interactive readability cannot be established from this output.",
  "strengths": [
    "Decomposition generally follows meaningful responsibilities. parsing separates identity, folding, graph interpretation, and evidence; contexts separates value semantics, source capture, retries, storage, and diagram binding; changes separates capture, mutation, review, acceptance, and persistence.",
    "Attachments usually identify precise concepts. diagrams connects central:55 to generated:5 for scoped patching; parsing connects central:57 to evidence:18 for explainNode; evals gives distinct entry points into shared peripherals for prompt pins, runtime execution, materialization, and assessment.",
    "workspace provides useful connections between peripherals: session:22 → records:9 → delivery:3 follows publication into record submission. source_evidence similarly connects evidence:19 to lsp:10 through the whole-file integrity gate.",
    "Several peripherals add valuable distinctions rather than merely expanding inventories: agent_access/retry:16–25 separates new command IDs from retained retry commands; persistence/commit:23–26 distinguishes write durability; static_analysis/lifetime:17–21 qualifies coverage; chat/retry:2–7 explains the limits of request deduplication."
  ],
  "issues": [
    {
      "evidence": "composition/central remains 99 lines, canvas_commands/central 92, and evals/central 96. Detailed material also appears twice: diagrams/central:60–73 and registry:2–20 both explain retention and deferred validation; persistence/central:37–47 and publish:2–21 both walk through capture and catalog publication; composition/central:50–70 and requests:2–22 repeat dispatch and error handling.",
      "impact": "The central diagrams still function partly as implementation references. Readers must scan substantial detail before deciding which peripheral to visit, while repeated descriptions enlarge the collection without providing equivalent new information.",
      "recommendation": "Give each central branch a concise responsibility, essential invariant, and handoff. Keep detailed ordering, exceptional cases, and validation lists primarily in their relevant peripherals. Preserve enough context for independent reading, but remove duplicated walkthroughs rather than relying on line-count reduction alone."
    },
    {
      "evidence": "agent_access/ids:22–23 says shared projection wiring is outside the supplied sources, while composition/context:20–21 explicitly depicts one CanvasApiProjection injected into both services. diagram_generation/gate:24 says submission implementation is outside its sources, while diagrams/central:49–59 explains generated submission. changes/accept:10–13 stops at an unavailable catalog implementation, while persistence/central:48–52 and publish:22–25 describe related publication operations and retry identities.",
      "impact": "Point-local evidence qualifications read like collection-wide knowledge gaps. The reader encounters repeated boundaries without being directed to the companion canvas that supplies additional evidence. These are presentation inconsistencies, not established source-level errors.",
      "recommendation": "Reconcile evidence scope across the collection. Where another point supplies relevant detail, qualify the limitation as local to this point and add a concise reference using its point and diagram IDs. Keep caller responsibilities locally and designate the companion point as the detailed explanation of the receiving component."
    },
    {
      "evidence": "canvas_commands/central:67 describes opening a view as stashing and resuming, but views:5–8 distinguishes the already-active branch, which only stashes. chat/central:65–68 presents settlement followed by publication and cleanup, while lifetime:12–15 says child-save rejection occurs before the cleanup finally. diagram_generation/central:34 broadly says 'finally clean up prompt session', while lifetime:6–9 distinguishes setup from the protected prompt region.",
      "impact": "Some overview statements lose qualifications that materially change their meaning. Readers who remain in the central diagram can infer stronger restoration or cleanup guarantees than the associated peripheral supports.",
      "recommendation": "Audit central summaries against their peripherals. Include short conditions where an exception changes the guarantee—for example, different-view restoration or cleanup around the prompt/publication attempt—and leave the full branch mechanics in the peripheral."
    },
    {
      "evidence": "rendering/project:19–24 produces the projected baseline, and settle:9 explicitly requires that baseline for supported clones; local:16–22 describes measurements consumed by route:4–8. Yet every rendering edge returns through central. Similarly, diagram_generation/gate:20–24 describes revision handoff and batch:11–19 describes staged application, but their relationship is represented only through separate central attachments.",
      "impact": "Connectivity is complete, but some important dependencies remain implicit in prose. The diagrams work well as expandable documentation sections while making readers reconstruct the transfers between those sections themselves.",
      "recommendation": "Add selective connections between peripherals where an explicit output, prerequisite, or handoff is already supported by the text. Label the transferred state or dependency precisely, and remove redundant central attachments if necessary to keep navigation clear. Do not add relationships merely to increase edge count."
    },
    {
      "evidence": "composition places central at column 0, context/evals/requests at column 125, and lifetime/rules at column 235. Its central:71 → lifetime:10 connection spans from approximately row 70 to row 9 across the intermediate diagram column. canvas_commands similarly connects central:48 to grid:1 at column 195, beyond the column-108 peripherals. The collection includes extents of 320×99 cells for composition and 319×102 for evals.",
      "impact": "Nonoverlapping text does not ensure easy navigation. Remote attachments and large vertical offsets increase scanning distance and require connector routes to negotiate intervening diagram regions. Exact crossings or label collisions would be later rendering outcomes and are not demonstrated here.",
      "recommendation": "Arrange peripherals around their attachment locations and common reading paths, reserving clear connector corridors. Reconsider the far-right placement of directly attached peripherals and align long-distance targets more closely with their source branches. Choose layouts independently for each canvas."
    },
    {
      "evidence": "static_analysis attaches central:10, 'Modular structure -- boundaries and visibility', to models:1, although models:7–18 also covers calls, ownership, and data dependencies. chat attaches central:78, 'Service disposal', to lifetime:1, whose opening branches describe ordinary turn completion, connection failure, and child settlement before service disposal at line 16.",
      "impact": "These endpoints are valid, but their source labels advertise only part of the destination's scope. Readers looking for other lifecycle paths or analytical models may not discover the relevant peripheral from the central concept they are reading.",
      "recommendation": "Match attachment scope to destination scope. Use a broader central concept where appropriate, or provide another precise entry into a distinct destination branch when it materially improves discovery. Preserve the targeted multi-entry approach already used effectively in evals."
    },
    {
      "evidence": "Several peripheral titles combine otherwise distinct reading tasks: diagram_generation/lifetime:2–14 covers model-session and retained-run ownership, then :15–22 switches to placement computation; parsing/identity:19–26 moves from identity/cache ownership into geometry projection and runtime freezing; static_analysis/lifetime:17–21 adds coverage accounting after compiler release.",
      "impact": "The detail is useful, but its location is not always predictable from the peripheral title. Readers must inspect entire trees to find information that belongs to a different conceptual question.",
      "recommendation": "Review peripheral cohesion after removing duplication. Rename a peripheral to accurately advertise its scope, regroup related branches, or split only where independent navigation justifies it. Retain different decompositions for different points rather than enforcing a common peripheral structure."
    }
  ],
  "regenerationInstructions": "Regenerate Phase 3 with collection-wide editorial coordination. Preserve the meaningful responsibility boundaries, precise attachments, useful exceptions, and connected nonoverlapping diagrams. Make central trees clearer entry maps by moving duplicated mechanics into their owning peripherals. Reconcile point-local evidence limitations with companion canvases and provide concise point/diagram references. Keep central guarantees consistent with detailed branches, improve a few important dependency connections and attachment scopes, and place peripherals according to reading paths and connector clearance. Verify line references after edits. Do not invent source facts, impose uniform branch counts or relationship categories, or claim rendered routing defects without rendering evidence."
}
